A Swing component is a reusable graphical object that represents a part of a user interface, such as a button, label, text field, or text area.
A JPanel is a lightweight container used to organize and group Swing components inside another container, such as a JFrame.

Component Hierarchy
JFrame
│
└── JPanel
      │
      ├── JLabel
      ├── JTextField
      ├── JTextArea
      └── JButton

Notice that: JFrame is the main window.
             JPanel organizes components.
             Components are added to the panel.
             The panel is then added to the frame.

What is JPanel?
A JPanel is simply a container.
It doesn't usually display information itself. Instead, it holds and organizes other components.
Example: JPanel panel = new JPanel();
To place it inside a window: frame.add(panel);

What is JLabel?
A JLabel displays text or an image.
Example: JLabel label = new JLabel("Student Name:");
Output: Student Name:
A label displays information—it does not accept user input.

What is JTextField?
A JTextField allows the user to enter a single line of text.
Example: JTextField field = new JTextField(15);
The number 15 specifies the preferred width in columns.
Example: Name:
         [_______________]
         What is JTextArea?
A JTextArea allows the user to enter multiple lines of text.
Example: JTextArea area = new JTextArea(5, 20);
The numbers represent: 5 rows, 20 columns
Example: Comments: __________________
                   __________________
                   __________________
                   __________________
                   __________________

What is JButton?
A JButton represents a clickable button.
Example: JButton button = new JButton("Submit");
Output: [ Submit ]
Today, the button won't do anything yet. In the next GUI lecture, we'll learn how to respond to button clicks using event listeners.

Adding Components
Suppose we create:

JPanel panel = new JPanel();
JLabel label = new JLabel("Name:");
JTextField field = new JTextField(15);
JButton button = new JButton("Save");

Now add them to the panel:

panel.add(label);
panel.add(field);
panel.add(button);

Finally:

frame.add(panel);

Everything appears inside the window.

Default Layout

Notice that we never specified where each component should appear.
That's because JPanel uses a FlowLayout by default.
FlowLayout arranges components from left to right.
Like words in a sentence: Name: [_______] [Save]
We'll study layouts in more detail later.

Complete Example
import javax.swing.*;

public class StudentForm {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        JFrame frame = new JFrame("Student Form");

        JPanel panel = new JPanel();

        JLabel label = new JLabel("Name:");

        JTextField field = new JTextField(15);

        JButton button = new JButton("Register");

        panel.add(label);

        panel.add(field);

        panel.add(button);

        frame.add(panel);

        frame.setSize(400, 200);

        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);

        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);

        frame.setVisible(true);

    }

}
Reading Text from a JTextField
Suppose the user types: Aba
To retrieve that text: String name = field.getText();
To change the text programmatically: field.setText("Professor Byte");
Writing to a JLabel
You can also change a label's text.
label.setText("Welcome!");
Writing to a JTextArea
area.setText("Java\nProgramming")
The \n creates a new line.

